Usage: ln [OPTION]... [FILE]...

Create links between files. By default, make hard links.

  -s, --symbolic         make symbolic links instead of hard links
  -f, --force            remove existing destination files
  -i, --interactive      prompt whether to remove destinations
  -b, --backup           make a backup of each existing destination
  -S, --suffix=SUFFIX    override the usual backup suffix
  -t, --target-directory=DIRECTORY  specify the target directory
  -T, --no-target-directory  treat LINK_NAME as a normal file
  -r, --relative         create links relative to link location

On Windows, hard links and symbolic links are supported.
Note: Creating symbolic links may require administrator privileges.

OPTIONS:
  -s, --symbolic              make symbolic links instead of hard links
  -f, --force                 remove existing destination files
  -v, --verbose               print name of each linked file
  -n, --no-dereference        treat LINK_NAME as a normal file if it is a
                              symbolic link to a directory
  -i, --interactive           prompt whether to remove destinations
  -L, --logical               dereference TARGETs that are symbolic links
  -P, --physical              make hard links directly to symbolic links
      --dereference           dereference TARGETs that are symbolic links
  -b, --backup                make a backup of each existing destination file
  -S, --suffix ARG            override the usual backup suffix
  -r, --relative              with -s, create links relative to link location
  -t, --target-directory ARG  specify the DIRECTORY in which to create the links
  -T, --no-target-directory   treat LINK_NAME as a normal file always
  -d, --directory             allow the hard link to be a directory (privileged)
  -F                          allow hard link to directory (alias for -d)
      --help                  display this help and exit
  -V, --version               output version information and exit

EXIT STATUS:
  0  if OK,
  1  if minor problems,
  2  if serious trouble.

WinuxCmd is a Windows implementation of GNU CoreUtils for Linux-Windows developers and AI coding assistants.
